{\b\fs30 Oath Light}\par
{\b End-User Notice, Disclosure & Privacy Policy}\par
Version 1.0.0 - 2026-08-01\par
\par
Please read this before installing. Installing Oath Light means you understand and agree to what it does, how it protects itself from being disabled, and what it can access on this device.\par
\par
{\b 1. This is an early release, and it has not been security-tested}\par
Version 1.0.0 is the first public build. No independent security review or red-team test has been done on it - everything this notice says about how well Oath Light resists being switched off is the authors' assessment of their own code, not an outside verdict. The app shows an "Early release" badge for this reason.\par
No content filter catches everything either. Oath Light may block the wrong thing, miss something it should have blocked, or stop working - and someone determined to get around it on their own machine eventually can. Do not rely on it as your only safeguard. It is provided "as is", with no warranty of any kind. It works best alongside people who know what you are trying to do.\par
\par
{\b 2. What Oath Light is}\par
Oath Light is an accountability and content-blocking tool designed to help you avoid pornography and other explicit material online. It has three parts:\par
- A browser extension that blocks known adult domains, a curated "graylist" of risky sites, and keyword matches - and can redirect you to a page of your choice.\par
- A desktop app that keeps that protection in place, checks that the extension stays active, and offers an optional, on-device AI monitor.\par
- A system-wide DNS filter that blocks the same lists for every application on this device, not just browsers. See section 4.\par
\par
{\b 3. Friction & tamper-resistance (please read - this is intentionally strict)}\par
Oath Light is deliberately hard to remove or disable in the moment, by design, so a momentary urge cannot instantly undo your commitment. Once installed:\par
- It starts automatically when you sign in to Windows and runs in the background (minimized). This is enforced, not an optional setting.\par
- It runs two cooperating processes - OathLight.exe and a hidden oathlightguard.exe - that watch each other. If one is closed or force-killed (including via Task Manager "End task"), the other restarts it. This is intentional behavior, not a virus.\par
- Some protections cannot be switched off at all. The uninstall guard, SafeSearch enforcement, YouTube Restricted Mode, the extension requirement and the DNS filter have no off switch anywhere in the app. Settings that are genuine choices weaken only after a 24-hour cool-off, during which protection stays fully on.\par
- Uninstalling is a two-step, delayed process. You request removal, then wait out a 24-hour cool-off before the app can actually be removed. Blocking stays fully active during the wait. You can cancel the request at any time.\par
- Windows' own uninstaller goes through that same wait. Removing Oath Light from Settings > Apps (or Add/Remove Programs) before the cool-off has elapsed is refused, and refusing changes nothing on your device - it does not half-remove the app. This is the only supported removal path besides the in-app one, and both honour the same timer.\par
- Microsoft Edge is held shut unless it is carrying the extension. Microsoft does not allow this extension to be force-installed into Edge on an ordinary consumer PC, so Oath Light closes that gap by closing Edge whenever it is running without the extension active. To fix it, open Oath Light and ask for a restore window: Edge is left alone for 90 seconds, opened straight at the page that installs the extension. The window does not extend, but you can ask for another one as many times as you need. Other browsers are never closed - they keep the extension installed by policy instead.\par
- Updating asks the same of you, briefly. A new version can only replace an old one while an update window is open, which you start from Settings inside the app. It lasts one minute, then protection comes back on its own whether or not the update happened.\par
These measures are friction, not unbreakable security: an administrator on this machine who sets out to remove Oath Light can still do so. They exist to slow down impulsive decisions - not to trap you.\par
\par
{\b 4. What it can access on this device}\par
To do its job, Oath Light:\par
- Reads your browsers' profile settings to check whether the Oath Light extension is installed and enabled.\par
- Adds Windows registry/policy entries to start at login, to register a local "native messaging host", and to keep the extension installed in supported browsers.\par
- Changes this device's DNS settings to point at a filtering resolver that runs locally on this machine (127.0.0.1). Lookups for blocked domains are refused; everything else is passed through to your normal upstream provider. Your original DNS settings are restored when Oath Light is properly uninstalled.\par
- Closes Microsoft Edge when it is running without the extension - see section 3. No other application is ever closed.\par
- Communicates between the app and the browser extension over your local machine only (localhost / 127.0.0.1).\par
- Optional AI monitor - only if you turn it on from Blocking Settings: periodically captures an image of your primary display and analyzes it on this device using built-in AI models to detect explicit imagery. This is off until you enable it.\par
\par
{\b 5. What it does NOT do - your privacy}\par
Oath Light is built to run entirely on your device. It does not:\par
- Send your browsing history, screenshots, or any personal data over the internet.\par
- Use any servers, cloud services, analytics, tracking, or telemetry.\par
- Require an account, login, email, or payment.\par
- Save screenshots or captured screen images to disk. Frames from the optional AI monitor are analyzed in memory; only a small preview is shown to you inside the app, and the frame is then discarded.\par
- Log your keystrokes or record what you type.\par
All processing - including the AI image analysis - happens locally. Nothing about what you view or do leaves this computer through Oath Light.\par
Two optional features you must turn on yourself are the only exceptions, and both use your account, not ours: the trusted-contact notifier (sends a short message saying that an event happened - never what was browsed) and the AI mentor (uses an API key you supply, sent to the provider you choose). Both are off by default.\par
\par
{\b 6. No warranty / limitation of liability}\par
Oath Light is free software provided free of charge, and is offered without warranty of any kind, express or implied. To the maximum extent permitted by law, the authors are not liable for any damages, data loss, missed or wrongful blocking, or other harm arising from its use. You install and use it at your own risk.\par
\par
{\b 7. Removing Oath Light}\par
You can remove Oath Light at any time by opening the app, going to Settings, and starting the uninstall request - then completing it after the cool-off period described in section 3. Windows' own Settings > Apps > Uninstall also works, but only once that same cool-off has elapsed; used earlier it refuses and leaves your device untouched. A completed removal restores your DNS settings and removes the browser policies, the autostart entries and the app's own data.\par
\par
{\b 8. Your agreement}\par
By continuing this installation you confirm that you have read and understood the above - including the tamper-resistance measures, the login autostart, the system DNS change, the closing of Microsoft Edge when it is unprotected, the optional on-device screen monitoring, and the delayed-uninstall process - and that you consent to Oath Light operating on this device as described.\par
\par
